WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.714
This video is about automated tests for systems whose answers vary from run to run.

00:00:05.914 --> 00:00:09.803
A teammate tweaks one line of the system prompt to fix a customer complaint.

00:00:10.053 --> 00:00:24.089
System prompt: The instructions you, the developer, put at the top of every request: who the model is, the rules, the output format. Users normally cannot see it. Like: The standing orders pinned on the wall of a control room.

00:00:24.389 --> 00:00:35.477
Prompt: Everything you send the model in one call: instructions, examples, data and the question. Like: The full test vector you feed a device under test.

00:00:35.777 --> 00:00:37.217
The complaint goes away.

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:44.584
Three other kinds of question quietly start failing, and nobody notices for two weeks. Evals are how you notice in two minutes.

00:00:44.834 --> 00:01:03.328
Eval: A repeatable test for an LLM system: fixed inputs, an expected result or a grading rule, and a score. Because outputs vary, you measure pass rates rather than one pass/fail. Like: A production test fixture that measures yield across a batch, not one unit.

00:01:04.428 --> 00:01:08.608
Evals are unit tests, adapted for software that is a little random.

00:01:09.008 --> 00:01:19.387
You keep a golden set: real inputs, each with something checkable. For example, must mention version 2.1.4, and must call the knowledge base search tool.

00:01:19.787 --> 00:01:32.303
Golden set: A curated list of example inputs with known-good answers (or grading notes) that you run on every change. Like: Golden reference captures you diff every new firmware build against.

00:01:32.603 --> 00:01:46.500
Tool: A function your program lets the model request: search, read a file, query a database, run a test. The model never runs it itself. Like: A button on a panel the model may ask the operator to press.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.154
Because outputs vary from run to run, you run each case several times.

00:01:51.554 --> 00:01:57.324
And you report pass rates instead of one pass or fail, the way you would burn in hardware over many cycles.

00:01:58.524 --> 00:02:02.030
There are three kinds of grader. Use the cheapest one that works.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:12.182
Code checks: does the answer contain this phrase, did the agent call this tool, did it avoid calling the dangerous one, is it short enough. Fast, free and exact.

00:02:12.582 --> 00:02:30.682
Agent: An LLM in a loop that decides its own next step: it calls tools, reads the results, and keeps going until the goal is met or a limit is hit. Like: A technician with a toolbox and a ticket, not a script: they choose which tool to use next based on what they just saw.

00:02:30.982 --> 00:02:38.842
An LLM judge, which is a model scoring answers against a written rubric, for qualities like helpfulness that code cannot check.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:54.057
LLM: Large language model: a model trained on a huge amount of text whose one skill is predicting the next piece of text. Chat assistants are built on it. Like: Phone autocomplete scaled up until it has read most of the public internet.

00:02:54.357 --> 00:03:04.678
Model: A trained file of numbers plus the math that uses them to turn an input into an output. For text, the input is your words and the output is a guess at the next word. Like: A compiled lookup engine: firmware image (the numbers) plus the code that reads it.

00:03:04.928 --> 00:03:16.619
Rubric: A written scoring guide: the specific criteria and what earns each score. Like: A test plan's pass criteria written down before anyone runs the test.

00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:21.575
And humans, to check that the judge agrees with people, and to review edge cases.

00:03:21.975 --> 00:03:31.785
LLM judges have known biases, for example favouring whichever answer is shown first. So ask twice with the order swapped, and only trust verdicts that agree.

00:03:32.985 --> 00:03:34.994
Here is how evals protect a team.

00:03:35.394 --> 00:03:38.180
Someone changes a prompt, a model or a tool.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:43.677
Continuous integration runs the golden set automatically, several trials per case.

00:03:44.077 --> 00:03:48.895
The results are compared with the last release, case by case, not only the average.

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:54.845
If quality drops, or any critical case fails even once, the gate fails the build.

00:03:55.245 --> 00:04:00.841
And every failure found in production becomes a new case, so the same bug cannot return quietly.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:12.640
Question for you: Overall eval score went up from 0.86 to 0.93, but one critical safety case dropped below 100 percent. Ship or block?

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:30.283
Answer: Block. Averages hide regressions. Critical cases, such as never flashing devices without approval, must pass every time, whatever the overall score says.

00:04:30.583 --> 00:04:43.888
Regression eval: Re-running the same eval set after every change (prompt, model, tool, code) and failing the build if the score drops. Like: A regression test suite in CI, but measuring pass rates.

00:04:44.988 --> 00:04:45.639
Recap.

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:49.638
Evals use a golden set, several trials, and pass rates.

00:04:50.038 --> 00:04:53.741
Use code graders first, and LLM judges with care.

00:04:54.141 --> 00:04:57.160
Check judges for position bias by swapping the order.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:16.322
Position / verbosity bias: Known judge errors: favouring whichever answer is shown first (position bias), favouring longer answers (verbosity bias), or favouring its own model family's style (self-preference). Like: A tired reviewer who prefers whichever patch they read first, or the longest one.

00:05:16.622 --> 00:05:21.567
And gate every release on per case results. Lab ten builds the harness and the gate.
